#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
NJ Real Estate Licensing Trainer -- Desktop App  (V4: the Learning Loop, audited)
=================================================
A native desktop window (Tkinter + CustomTkinter) around an adaptive study
engine. The conventional layer is standard psychometrics done carefully:
3PL IRT ability estimation, Bayesian knowledge tracing with forgetting,
FSRS-6 scheduling, CD-CAT item selection, and DINA per-skill diagnosis
across 172 sub-skills.

What is less usual is the second layer, which measures the MEASUREMENT.

  DISTINGUISHABILITY.  Most adaptive systems treat the item bank as ground
  truth and attribute every miss to the learner. This one asks whether the
  items can separate the states it is trying to tell apart -- distractor
  discrimination, near-duplicate items, unidentifiable skill profiles, and
  psychometrically erratic items -- and routes differently when they
  cannot. An unresolved finding is a routing key, not a dashboard warning:
  a structural failure bypasses drilling entirely, because more practice on
  items that provably cannot distinguish two profiles will raise confidence
  without raising validity.

  GOVERNANCE.  Every adaptive policy is a versioned artefact. Candidates are
  proposed from a visible slice of history and judged on a sealed slice the
  proposer never sees; promotion is staged by an evidence rung and limited
  to a canary share until it earns full release; rollback is one pointer
  update. This exists because the feedback loops were, at one point,
  mutating live behaviour with no gate, no version and no way back.

  PROVENANCE.  Every claim carries how it was established -- authored,
  measured, or inferred -- and says so at the point of use rather than in a
  footnote. Content is ASSEMBLED from reviewed material, never generated:
  generation is available but gated behind a blind solver that never sees
  the answer key, plus human approval, and refuses rather than inventing.

  THE LEARNER CAN ARGUE BACK.  The model is open and disputable. A challenge
  does not overwrite a belief -- it schedules a probe at the disputed
  difficulty and lets evidence settle it, with "inconclusive" as a real
  verdict.

  REPAIR HAS STATE.  A diagnosed misconception is a hypothesis with a
  lifecycle, not a verdict. It is repaired only after four gates including a
  delayed retrieval that cannot be earned in the same sitting, and a belief
  that fails that check is REOPENED rather than quietly left open.

Refusal is treated as a feature throughout: the app abstains rather than
guessing when evidence is thin, declines to generate what it cannot verify,
and blocks its own promotions on insufficient data.

Shares the same local database as the CLI trainer (~/.nj_re_trainer/progress.db),
so progress carries over between the two if you use both.

Requires: Python 3.12+ (f-string expressions with backslashes), and the `customtkinter` package:
    pip install customtkinter

Everything beyond that is OPTIONAL and additive. The app runs fully offline
on the line above alone, and every model-backed feature degrades to a
working tier-0 floor rather than disappearing. Settings -> Improvement
Review -> "Setup: libraries and models" shows these same commands in-app,
with a button that copies the whole set.

    # scheduling + accurate hardware probe (~20 MB)
    pip install fsrs psutil

    # semantic + retrieval (~2 GB). The CPU wheel is deliberate: the default
    # torch index pulls CUDA and is several GB larger for no benefit here.
    pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
    pip install sentence-transformers "transformers>=4.44" accelerate

    # deterministic measurement layer (~150 MB). girth is BUILD-TIME only.
    pip install numpy scipy scikit-learn girth

    # local generation, 16 GB+ RAM
    pip install llama-cpp-python "huggingface_hub[cli]"

Models, by the worker each one plays. Pre-fetch them: a first-use download
inside a study session stalls for minutes with no progress signal.

    hf download Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B                       # router
    hf download Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B             # retrieval
    hf download BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5                # 33M CPU fallback
    hf download cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L6-v2   # reranker
    hf download MoritzLaurer/DeBERTa-v3-base-mnli     # entailment
    hf download HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM3-3B              # confuser
    hf download microsoft/Phi-4-mini-instruct         # differentiator

The confuser and the differentiator MUST be different models. Neither may
grade its own output -- a generator asked to check its own item confirms it
essentially always, and that is the failure the candidate bank and the
blind solver exist to prevent.

Deliberately NOT dependencies: py-irt and pyBKT. Both estimate parameters
from a response matrix across MANY learners; with a single learner they
would fit one person's idiosyncrasies and report them as calibrated. The
built-in shrinkage estimator is more appropriate here precisely because it
is honest about how little one response vector can support.
"""

import base64
import json
import hashlib
import uuid
import zlib
import platform
import math
import os
import random
