# ===========================================================================
# V8.2 -- THE VISUAL LEARNING SURFACE: GRAMMAR, GATHERERS, PRESENTERS
# ===========================================================================
# The contract (directive II) decides WHAT may reach the learner: words, tiers,
# counts and dates, never internal continuous variables. V8 obeyed that by
# rendering text. V8.2 keeps the same boundary and renders STRUCTURE: where a
# skill sits in the course, how it is moving, what today's session is built
# from, why it was chosen, and what changed. Everything in this block is either
#   * a GATHERER, the only place engine state is read, or
#   * a PRESENTER, a pure function from gathered state to learner vocabulary,
# so every picture drawn later is a picture of a presenter's output, and the
# catalogue can assert on the presenters without a display.
#
# Design rules, each enforced by a catalogue probe:
#   1. Position encodes curriculum, never state. A skill's place on the map is
#      a function of its id and its domain only, so the learner can build a
#      spatial memory of the course that learning does not rearrange.
#   2. Fill encodes the ladder; rings encode completion; overlays encode what
#      is asked of the learner now. Every tier has a distinct SHAPE signature,
#      so nothing depends on colour alone.
#   3. Red never encodes the learner. Low progress is a place on the map.
#   4. No presenter emits a decimal, a percentage or an engine term.
#   5. Comparisons between actions are descriptive (what a session would
#      contain, what would wait), never predictive.
#   6. Motion explains a transition and is skipped under reduced motion.

VIS_TIER_ORDER = (TIER_NOT_STARTED, TIER_ATTEMPTED, TIER_FAMILIAR, TIER_BUILDING,
                  TIER_STEADY, TIER_STRONG, TIER_REVIEW_SOON, TIER_READY_FOR_SIMULATION)

# fill: share of the disc filled (the ladder); ring: completed the ladder;
# outer: ready for simulation; clock: due now; dash: nothing recorded yet.
VIS_GLYPH = {
    TIER_NOT_STARTED:          {"fill": 0.0,  "ring": False, "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": True},
    TIER_ATTEMPTED:            {"fill": 0.25, "ring": False, "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": False},
    TIER_FAMILIAR:             {"fill": 0.5,  "ring": False, "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": False},
    TIER_BUILDING:             {"fill": 0.75, "ring": False, "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": False},
    TIER_STEADY:               {"fill": 1.0,  "ring": False, "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": False},
    TIER_STRONG:               {"fill": 1.0,  "ring": True,  "outer": False, "clock": False, "dash": False},
    TIER_REVIEW_SOON:          {"fill": 1.0,  "ring": True,  "outer": False, "clock": True,  "dash": False},
    TIER_READY_FOR_SIMULATION: {"fill": 1.0,  "ring": True,  "outer": True,  "clock": False, "dash": False},
}

# ---- V8.3: resolution by zoom level -------------------------------------------
# The engine keeps eight tiers. The picture shows four coarse states at course
# and area level, and the full eight-tier word only in the skill inspector, so
# perceptual resolution rises only when the learner asks for it. Four is a
# design hypothesis to be tested with learners, not a perceptual constant.
